# Gitleaks false positives.
#
# Fingerprint format: <commit>:<filepath>:<rule-id>:<line>
#
# All entries below are .NET assembly PublicKeyToken values, which are NOT secrets:
# a PublicKeyToken is the truncated SHA-1 hash of an assembly's strong-name *public*
# key. It exists to be published — the CLR needs it to resolve assembly references,
# so every consumer of a signed assembly must know it. The private key never appears
# in an assembly or in config.
#
# The specific value flagged here, b77a5c561934e089, is Microsoft's own well-known
# token identifying Microsoft-signed framework assemblies. It appears verbatim in
# Microsoft's documentation and in millions of public .csproj and .config files. It
# authenticates nothing and grants no access.
#
# Gitleaks matches it because "PublicKeyToken=<16 hex chars>" has the shape of a
# generic "...Token=<high-entropy string>" credential assignment.
#
# These values cannot simply be deleted: the EF6 <section> registration and the
# assembly <Reference> elements require fully-qualified type names that include the
# PublicKeyToken. Removing them breaks the build and the runtime config binding.
#
# All three were introduced in 86f238b, the unmodified upstream import from
# dotnet-architecture/eShopModernizing.

